  "worked_solution": "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=\u27e8x(t),y(t)\u27e9$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{{x}^{\u2032}(t){y}^{\u2033}(t)-{y}^{\u2032}(t){x}^{\u2033}(t)}{([{x}^{\u2032}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{\u2032}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t,\\,y(t)=t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(t) & =-{t}^{-1},\\, & {y}^{\u2032}(t) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{\u2033}(t) & ={t}^{-2},\\, & {y}^{\u2033}(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(t) & =\\frac{-{t}^{-1}\\cdot 0-1\\cdot ({t}^{-2})}{{((-{t}^{-1})^{2}+(1)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{t}^{-2}}{{({t}^{-2}+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{t}^{-2}\\cdot ({t}^{2})^{3/2}}{{(1+{t}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{t}^{-2}\\cdot |t{|}^{3}}{{(1+{t}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{t}^{-2}\\cdot {t}^{3}}{{(1+{t}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-t}{\\sqrt{(1+{t}^{2})^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we removed the absolute value because $t>0.$",
